-- EXPECT: box nesting deeper than 512
-- a box that contains itself is constructible through mutate; the walk is depth-bounded
42 box box 'b let b (drop b) mutate (drop) lend free

-- EXPECT: 'lend' body may not 'let'-bind the snapshot when the box contains a box — the snapshot copies the pointer, not the contents, so a later 'mutate' would free it while the binding still refers to it. Read it out with `k peek` instead of binding it.
-- box-in-box: the snapshot shares the inner box by pointer
42 box box 'ob let
ob ('inner let ob (99 box) mutate free inner free 1) lend swap free drop

-- ===== aliasing and linearity =====
-- EXPECT: cannot 'let'-bind a dict as 'd' — a dict is a heap object and the binding would alias it, so dropping either copy leaves the other reading freed memory. Thread the dict on the stack instead (see examples/kv-server.slap), or use a record if you want a value you can bind.
-- a let-bound dict would alias the one on the stack
-- rather than failing. kv-server.slap:14 documented the stack-threading
-- discipline in a comment; now the checker enforces it.
dict "a" "b" insert 'd let d drop d "a" of must drop

-- EXPECT: 'insert' cannot embed a linear value into a stackable container — linearity would be lost
-- a tagged box stays linear, so insert's guard still sees it
dict "k" 42 box 'x tag insert drop

-- EXPECT: 'drop' requires copyable value, got linear type
-- the same laundering, one step earlier: dropping a tagged box leaked it
42 box 'x tag drop

-- EXPECT: 'dup' requires copyable value, got linear type
-- and `dup` on one produced two references to a single BoxData
42 box 'x tag dup drop drop

-- EXPECT: 'push' cannot embed a linear value into a stackable container — linearity would be lost
-- a list is as stackable as a dict; push has to reject it for the same reason
list 42 box 'x tag push drop

-- EXPECT: box 'b' was already consumed on line 1 — a Box binding names one heap cell no matter how many times it is looked up, so consuming through it twice is a double free. 'lend' and 'mutate' hand the same box back, so freeing what they return consumes the binding too
-- a Box binding names one heap cell, so only one lookup may retire it
42 box 'b let b free b free

-- EXPECT: box 'b' was already consumed on line 1 — a Box binding names one heap cell no matter how many times it is looked up, so consuming through it twice is a double free. 'lend' and 'mutate' hand the same box back, so freeing what they return consumes the binding too
-- `lend` and `mutate` hand the same box back, so freeing what they return
-- consumes the binding too -- the alternative reading frees one cell twice
42 box 'b let b (drop 1) lend drop free b free

